            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Shuffles a list into a random order: every item kept, each exactly once, in a new order every time. Given a whole number n instead, it shuffles 0 to n-1.\n\nUse it for a random running order of scenes, a play order of clips that repeats none until all have played, a random assignment of performers to parts, or a shuffled set of positions to take values from (with np.take).\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.random.permutation\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA list or array to shuffle - for a 2-D array, whole rows are shuffled - or a single whole number n, for the numbers 0 to n-1 shuffled (a message box sends 8 as a list of one; that counts as n too). Every input is shuffled and sent.\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) draws different numbers every time the patch opens. Any other number starts the same sequence every time - the same \"random\" piece on every run, which is what you want for rehearsal, comparison and debugging.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nshuffled:\nThe same items in a random order.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: the words do re mi fa sol in a new order every second. Bottom: 8, which makes the numbers 0 to 7 in a random order - a set of positions for np.take.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.random.choice picks some at random, possibly with repeats. np.sort and np.argsort put things back in order. np.take reorders by a list of positions.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Shuffles a list into a random order: every item kept, each exactly once, in a new order every time. Given a whole number n instead, it shuffles 0 to n-1.\n\nUse it for a random running order of scenes, a play order of clips that repeats none until all have played, a random assignment of performers to parts, or a shuffled set of positions to take values from (with np.take).\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.random.permutation\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA list or array to shuffle - for a 2-D array, whole rows are shuffled - or a single whole number n, for the numbers 0 to n-1 shuffled (a message box sends 8 as a list of one; that counts as n too). Every input is shuffled and sent.\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) draws different numbers every time the patch opens. Any other number starts the same sequence every time - the same \"random\" piece on every run, which is what you want for rehearsal, comparison and debugging.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nshuffled:\nThe same items in a random order.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: the words do re mi fa sol in a new order every second. Bottom: 8, which makes the numbers 0 to 7 in a random order - a set of positions for np.take.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.random.choice picks some at random, possibly with repeats. np.sort and np.argsort put things back in order. np.take reorders by a list of positions."
        },
